{"qid":"Q262","names":["People's Democratic Republic of Algeria"],"title":"People's Democratic Republic of Algeria","endonym":{"native":"الجزائر","lang":"ar"},"capsule":"Algeria gained independence from France on 5 July 1962 after an eight-year liberation war (1954–1962) and was formally proclaimed a people's democratic republic under the FLN (National Liberation Front), which governed as a single-party state from 1963 through 1989. The first president, Ahmed Ben Bella, was overthrown by Houari Boumediene in a 1965 coup; Boumediene nationalized the hydrocarbon sector in 1971 and guided a state-directed industrialisation programme until his death in 1978. A brief democratic opening in 1989–1991 collapsed when the army cancelled elections the Islamic Salvation Front (FIS) was poised to win, triggering a bloody civil war (1991–2002) that killed an estimated 150,000 people before the state restored control. Algeria remains the largest country in Africa by area, a major natural gas and oil exporter, and has been governed since 2019 by President Abdelmadjid Tebboune.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Post-independence national boundaries are precisely defined by treaty and are surveyed; territorial control during the 1991–2002 civil war is well documented, though some southern Saharan zones had ambiguous effective administration.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Algeria","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Algeria"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Algeria","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algeria"}],"britannica":"place/Algeria","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Algeria","rulers":[{"qid":"Q193043","name":